<h1>Configuration</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Defaults &lt; user config &lt; allowed project policy &lt; CLI flags.</p>

<h2>Project policy: <code>.vexesrc.json</code></h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="pun">{</span>
  <span class="str">"ecosystems"</span>: [<span class="str">"npm"</span>, <span class="str">"pypi"</span>],
  <span class="str">"severity"</span>: <span class="str">"high"</span>,
  <span class="str">"ignore"</span>: [],
  <span class="str">"analyze"</span>: { <span class="str">"signals"</span>: { <span class="str">"NO_REPOSITORY"</span>: <span class="str">"off"</span> } },
  <span class="str">"output"</span>: { <span class="str">"color"</span>: <span class="str">"auto"</span>, <span class="str">"format"</span>: <span class="str">"text"</span> }
<span class="pun">}</span></div>
<p>Repository config may set reporting policy: ecosystems, severity, ignores, strict/verbose/top, the deprecated reachability field, signal switches, and output color/format. It cannot enable sandbox execution, deep sampling, fix output, AI, or stale-cache use, and it cannot select cache storage or TTLs.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Project config is untrusted policy input.</strong> A checked-out branch can change what an ordinary report displays. Use <code>--no-project-config</code> for enforcement unless repository-owned policy is intentionally trusted.</div>

<h2>User policy</h2>
<p><code>~/.config/vexes/config.json</code> may set the reporting keys plus ordinary cache directory/TTL settings. Operational modes still require explicit CLI flags. Use <code>--no-user-config</code> when a run must not vary with runner-account policy.</p>

<h2>Selected options</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Values / behavior</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>ecosystems</code></td><td><code>npm</code>, <code>pypi</code>, <code>cargo</code>, <code>go</code>, <code>ruby</code>, <code>php</code>, <code>nuget</code>, <code>java</code>, <code>hex</code>, <code>pub</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>severity</code></td><td><code>critical</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>moderate</code>, <code>low</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>analyze.signals</code></td><td>Every documented analysis signal may be set to <code>"off"</code> for ordinary reporting</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>cache.advisoryTtlMs</code></td><td>Default 1 hour; clamped to at most 7 days</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>cache.metadataTtlMs</code></td><td>Historical name for analysis-signal TTL; default 24 hours, max 30 days</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>output.format</code></td><td><code>text</code>, <code>json</code>, or <code>sarif</code> where supported</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Guard clears configured ignores and signal suppression before deciding an install. This prevents repository policy from silencing guard evidence, but it does not make guard a complete package-safety boundary.</p>

<h2>AI provider variables</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>VEXES_AI_BASE</code>, optional <code>VEXES_AI_KEY</code>, and model variables for an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.</li>
  <li><code>ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL</code> plus <code>ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN</code> or <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> for a compatible endpoint.</li>
  <li><code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> alone for the hosted Anthropic API.</li>
</ul>
<p>Remote endpoints receive sanitized summary data when the AI feature is explicitly invoked. No provider is contacted merely because a key is absent or present without an AI flag/command.</p>
